      "text": "Textile wastewater usually contains a variety of organic pollutants, including surfactants, dyes, phenols, organic acids, and residues of fin - ishing chemicals used in textile treatment [16] . These substances contribute to the overall organic load and can affect the rejection of PFAS through their interactions with the membrane or with PFAS molecules in solution. The presence and concentration of these organic compounds can be observed in the physico-chemical analysis of the water, as shown in Fig. 2 , where parameters such as COD, TOC, SAC, and the concentration of surfactants and phenols provide information on the Fig. 3. EEM fluorescence spectra of a) TWW and b) UF permeate, and c) RO permeate. I. \u00b4 Curi \u00b4c et al. Journal of Environmental Chemical Engineering 13 (2025) 118608 5 composition of the organic compounds. Organic compounds in textile wastewater can also contribute to membrane fouling, which leads to the formation of an additional layer on the membrane surface. This fouling layer, which consists of organics such as surfactants, dyes and other dissolved organic compounds, can act as an additional separation bar - rier and potentially improve separation performance by reducing membrane pore size and increasing steric hindrance [17] . In some cases, this effect can enhance the rejection of certain impurities, including"
