      "text": "Results and discussion 3.1. Removal of organic and inorganic indicators in TWW In order to evaluate the influence of the water matrix on the removal of PFAS concentrations, both organic and inorganic indicators (COD, turbidity, TN b , SAC, EC, phenols, anionic and non-ionic surfactants, sulfate and chloride concentration) were investigated in TWW and permeates. These indicators are relevant as high organic and inorganic loading can reduce membrane lifetime, i.e., primarily permeability (flux), and impair PFAS rejection by altering membrane interactions, pore fouling, or competitive transport through the membrane [13] . In addition, fluorescence spectroscopy was performed to further charac - terize the organics present in TWW and permeates. The fluorescence spectra obtained provide information on the nature and composition of dissolved organic compounds that may affect the rejection of PFAS [14] . These results complement the physico-chemical analyses and are shown in Fig. 2 and Fig. 3 . The results indicate a significant reduction in organic and inorganic indicators during the membrane treatment process, as shown by the physico-chemical parameters and fluorescence spectroscopy analyzes. The concentrations of phenols, anionic and non-ionic surfactants in TWW were significantly reduced after UF and further minimized after RO, proving the high efficiency of membrane treatment in removing these contaminants."
