      "text": "their chemical structure, especially the strong carbon-fluorine bonds, makes them very resistant to degradation and they persist in the envi - ronment and in the human body. For this reason, they are often referred to as persistent chemicals [2] . The U.S. Environmental Protection Agency first warned against the use of PFAS in textiles and cosmetics in 2001 [3] . Also in 2001, certain PFAS, PFOA, and PFOS were included in the Stockholm Convention on Persistent Organic Pollutants, which prohibits or strictly regulates their use [4] . Certain standards in the textile industry, such as the Global Organic Textile Standard and the OEKO-TEX Standard 100, have also restricted the use of PFAS [2] . Some textile manufacturers still use PFAS in production, but the amount has been significantly reduced. However, despite this reduction, some PFAS still end up in textile wastewater (TWW), which must be treated before being discharged into public sewage systems or natural waters. Conventional biological treatment methods such as activated sludge, are ineffective in removing PFAS due to their resistance to microbial degradation [5] . Various wastewater treatment technologies have been explored for PFAS removal, including adsorption, ion exchange, advanced oxidation processes (AOPs), and pressure driven membrane processes [6] . Adsorption processes using activated carbon in powder or"
