      "section": "results",
      "text": "3.2. PFAS removal efficiency using membrane separation The analysis of 34 PFAS compounds revealed that 11 of them were detected in the TWW, as shown in Table 2 . These concentrations of detected PFAS were as expected, considering that the textile industry studied primarily processes cotton-based materials, which generally have a lower need for PFAS in production, compared to synthetic tex - tiles, where PFAS are more commonly used for water and stain resis - tance. The presence of these detected PFAS compounds can be attributed to auxiliary chemicals, processing residues or external sources of contamination (PFAS-contaminated water supply, cross-contamination from equipment and facilities, auxiliary chemicals containing traces of PFAS, airborne deposits, PFAS-treated workwear or residual contami - nation in raw materials such as dyes and auxiliaries). The efficiency of PFAS removal by UF and RO was evaluated, examining their removal capacities and the impact of the water matrix on separation performance. As mentioned above, 11 PFAS were detected in TWW, but for five of them (PFHpA, PFPeS, 9Cl-PF3ONS, PFHxDA, and N-MeFOSE) the con - centration was lower than the LOQ value for the influent ( Table 2 ) and for two of them (PFHxA and 6:2 FTS) the detected concentrations were slightly lower than the LOQ values."
